Fix prompt: payments.invoice_id NOT NULL crash on job payments

The bug

POST /api/jobs/<id>/payments throws a 500:

sqlite3.IntegrityError: NOT NULL constraint failed: payments.invoice_id


whenever a payment is recorded against a job that has no direct invoice_id (job-linked payments only set job_id, per add_job_payment() in services/jobs.py).

Root cause

models.py declares Payment.invoice_id as nullable=True, but the actual SQLite table was created before that column allowed nulls, and still enforces NOT NULL at the database level. SQLite's ALTER TABLE ... ADD COLUMN (used everywhere in schema_migrations.py) cannot loosen an existing column's constraint — that requires rebuilding the table.

What to do

Add a new migration function to backend/app/schema_migrations.py, following the same idempotent style as the existing ensure_job_invoice_schema() and ensure_prompt4_schema() functions in that file, that:





Checks whether payments.invoice_id is currently NOT NULL (inspect the column's nullable flag via SQLAlchemy's inspect(db.engine).get_columns("payments"), not just whether the column exists — this is a constraint-level fix, not a missing-column fix).



If it is still NOT NULL, rebuild the payments table with the corrected schema:





Create a new table (e.g. payments_new) with the exact same columns as the current Payment model in models.py, but with invoice_id genuinely nullable.



Copy all existing rows from payments into payments_new.



Drop the old payments table.



Rename payments_new to payments.



Recreate any indexes that existed on the original table (invoice_id, job_id are both indexed per the model).



Wrap the whole rebuild in a single transaction so a failure midway doesn't leave the database half-migrated.



Add this new function to run_full_upgrade() in the same file, in the correct order (after ensure_job_invoice_schema(), since that's what introduces job_id in the first place — the rebuild should happen once both columns are known to exist).



Do not modify models.py — it's already correct. This is purely a database-schema catch-up migration.

